<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivo Andrić</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1892">1892</a>–<a href="/years/1975">1975</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivo Andrić was a Yugoslav novelist and diplomat born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>. His novels, above all The Bridge on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drina">Drina</a> (1945), trace the history of Bosnia under Ottoman and Austrian rule. He served as a diplomat of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, including as envoy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1939–1941, and received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1961.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivo-andric.jpg" alt="Ivo Andrić" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stevan Kragujević · CC BY-SA 3.0 rs · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S._Kragujevic,_Ivo_Andric,_1961_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ivan Andrić was born on 9 October 1892 in the village of Dolac, near Travnik, while his mother, Katarina, was visiting relatives. His father, Antun, a silversmith who worked as a school janitor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarajevo">Sarajevo</a>, died of tuberculosis when Andrić was two, and the boy was raised in Višegrad by an aunt and uncle. A scholarship took him in 1902 to the Great Sarajevo Gymnasium, whose curriculum aimed to produce supporters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg Monarchy</a>, which he disliked. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a> in 1908 he became involved in South Slav youth movements. In late 1911 he was elected first president of the Serbo-Croat Progressive Movement, and he joined Young Bosnia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Archduke Franz Ferdinand was assassinated in June 1914 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gavrilo-princip">Gavrilo Princip</a>, a friend of Andrić, he was in Split, and he was arrested in late July or early August for anti-state activities. Held in several prisons, he spent much of the war under house arrest and was released under an amnesty in July 1917. He studied South Slavic history and literature at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> and took his doctorate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a> in 1924, with a dissertation on spiritual life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> under Ottoman rule, which characterised the Ottoman occupation as negative. He entered the diplomatic service of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, working in it from 1920 to 1923 and again from 1924 to 1941, and in 1939 became ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><p>His tenure ended in April 1941 with the German-led invasion. He returned to occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> and lived quietly in a friend's apartment for the rest of the war, in conditions some biographers liken to house arrest, writing some of his most important works. Na <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drin">Drini</a> ćuprija (The Bridge on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drina">Drina</a>) appeared in March 1945, followed by Travnička hronika that September and Gospođica that November. The first chronicles the Mehmed Paša Sokolović Bridge and the town of Višegrad from the 16th century until the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>; the second follows a French diplomat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 26 October 1961 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-academy">Swedish Academy</a> awarded Andrić the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature, citing the epic force with which he traced themes and depicted human destinies drawn from his country's history. He gave all the prize money, some 30 million dinars, to buy library books in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> on 13 March 1975. The apartment where he spent the war became a museum, and the Ivo Andrić Foundation supports study of his work. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>'s only Nobel laureate in literature he was widely respected there, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> regards him highly, while Bosniak critics have disparaged his works for their supposed anti-Muslim bias.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ivo Andrić” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Belgrade, Zagreb and Ljubljana nonetheless grew as modern cities, and writers such as <b>Ivo Andrić</b> and Miroslav Krleža and the sculptor Ivan Meštrović were active in the period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ivo Andrić</b> won the Nobel Prize for Literature in 1961; Yugoslavia entered the Eurovision Song Contest the same year and won it in 1989 with Riva's 'Rock Me'.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dolac, in what is now <a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.22,17.77&amp;year=1945" aria-label="Dolac on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4975 599 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ivo Andrić"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5425 869h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5425 869h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.22,17.77&amp;year=1945">What was here in 1945? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
